WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.280
SAMRAT RAY: Hi,
today we are going

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.870
to talk about how you can evolve
to a zero trust security model

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:13.520
with Anthos.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.010
I am Samrat Ray.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:18.770
I am the product management
lead for Anthos security

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:19.880
at Google Cloud.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.800
And with me is Sandra.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.330
SANDRA GUO: My
name is Sandra Guo.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:27.570
I'm a security product
manager for Google Cloud.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.490
SAMRAT RAY: And today
we'll talk about how

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.740
you can implement zero
trust security with Anthos.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:36.090
But before we talk
about how you can do it,

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.890
let's first talk about
what is zero trust.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.900
Then we talk to folks
outside of Google,

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.580
we hear many
different definitions

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.200
of what is zero trust.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.750
We hear terms like, it's micro
or even nano segmentation

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.160
done at the workload level.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.720
We have heard terms
like, perimeterless.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.340
We have heard somebody say
it's something as narrow

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.360
as, hey, I have
implemented mTLS,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.400
therefore I am doing zero trust.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.970
We have heard definitions
from vendors and others

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.130
saying things like
trust endpoint

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:15.060
and not the network, continuous
track monitoring, all of this

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.640
is zero trust.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.420
So in order to make
sense of this term,

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:24.500
we decided that we will give
it a clear definition rooted

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:28.790
in certain sets of principles
as we understand it at Google,

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:32.100
and then explain how
we're implementing

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:37.010
parts of those
principles using Anthos.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:40.550
So how do we think about
the term zero trust?

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.720
The first way of
thinking about it

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.050
is to think about the
term trust, right.

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:50.427
And instead of focusing
on the word zero,

00:01:50.427 --> 00:01:52.760
because we all understand--
there's a bit of a marketing

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.070
spin there--

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:58.780
we think about the
reducing privilege or trust

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.240
on all production
entities, right,

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:06.050
with respect to data, critical
workloads, and infrastructure.

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:09.710
So what we are saying is, we
don't trust any one thing.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:12.040
It's not just about
network security.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.570
It is bigger than that.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:15.550
Rather than that,
we think about it

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.250
as a series of controls
for defense in depth.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:21.730
And those controls include
infrastructure security,

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:24.510
border deployment security,
production network access,

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.130
and employee or insider access.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.560
We have described
our approach that

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.790
covers all of these dimensions
through a paper called

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:35.350
BeyondProd.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:38.200
And this is available
for you to read.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.470
We highly recommend that
you take a look that.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:45.060
Now let's talk about how we
can take these principles

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.440
and implement them with Anthos.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.810
Before we dive into
the how, let's talk

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.970
about another aspect, why.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:57.330
Why is this approach
suddenly relevant to you?

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.990
The reason for this being
important and gaining

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.870
more and more relevance
with our customers

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.280
is the confluence of
three technology trends.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:10.200
The first is a hybrid
and multi-cloud.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:15.870
We're seeing increasingly that
the production environment

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:19.530
is changing from a single
homogeneous production

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:23.220
environment, right, without
perhaps a single network,

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.910
to a distributed production
environment, spanning

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.970
multiple clouds,
and on-premises,

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:32.100
with different
network architectures,

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:37.520
and not having a consistent way
of viewing network security.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.470
This means that the traditional
parameter-based security

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:45.230
approaches no longer apply
as well as they did before.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:51.240
Second is the adoption of
containers and of Kubernetes.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.880
The adoption of
containers, in particular,

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:57.270
implies that your
traditional security that

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.810
was based upon securing
the host and trusting

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:04.980
the host, i.e., at the
infrastructure level,

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:09.000
is no longer sufficient
because the strong correlation

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.930
between a workload
and its host is

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.840
no longer true with Kubernetes.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.810
Therefore, in addition to
securing your infrastructure,

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:20.640
you now need to
rethink about how

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.970
you're securing your
workloads that are deployed

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.050
to this infrastructure.

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.390
There many of
those workloads may

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.090
be deployed on the same node.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:32.910
And a single workload may be
deployed on multiple nodes

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.310
throughout this level.

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:37.950
The last dimension
is that option

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.750
of service-based architectures.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:45.720
So we are seeing the breaking
of monoliths into microservices.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.810
This is great from a developer
velocity perspective.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.720
The challenge being, we
have now suddenly increase

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.230
the surface area of access
within your production

00:04:55.230 --> 00:05:00.360
network by several
magnitudes, thus you now

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:03.630
need to ensure that you are
properly securing access

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:08.150
to sensitive infrastructure
and to sensitive data

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:11.370
within your production
network, and not just relying

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.820
on [INAUDIBLE] controls
that you had before.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.010
So all of these
trends together mean

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.920
that we need to revisit
how we are doing security.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.890
And zero trust is a
great way of thinking

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:27.270
about how security can
appropriately apply

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:31.020
in this modern environment.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:33.250
So how can Anthos help?

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:37.570
Before we talk about
what Anthos does for you,

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:41.230
just want to set the context
that in this presentation,

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:44.920
we are only going to talk
about zero trust approaches

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:48.310
to production workload security.

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:50.170
We're not going to talk
about infrastructure

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:52.240
security or parameter security.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:55.840
You can apply zero trust
approaches to those dimensions

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.220
as well, we're just not
covering them today.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:01.200
We're going to focus
on workload security.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.870
So with Anthos, we enable
you to think about zero trust

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:06.890
along three macro principles.

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:09.670
The first being, only trusted
code and configuration

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:11.500
can be applied to production.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:15.542
The second is that
trust is not associated

00:06:15.542 --> 00:06:17.500
with just the network
location of the workload,

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:21.070
but is actually associated
with who the workload is

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:25.060
and the user, on behalf of
which the workload is accessing

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:27.640
any other system
on the production.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:31.620
The third is to make sure
that all service access is

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:36.630
granted to the right peer
and under the right request

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:38.020
context.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.820
So now we'll talk about
how you can actually

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:45.520
implement these principles in
your production environment

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:47.310
with Anthos technologies.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:49.530
And with that, I'll
hand it over to Sandra

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:55.110
to walk you through the
Anthos workload lifecycle.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:56.660
SANDRA GUO: Thank you, Samrat.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:00.140
If we take a look at
workload lifecycle,

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:04.070
it can roughly be
categorized into two phases,

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:09.540
so deploy phase, and the runtime
phase, with production identity

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:15.460
acting as a linchpin that
ties together the two phases.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:19.290
So provenances can be tied--

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:21.060
so what is the right
production identity?

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:24.510
It can tie together as a
provenance to the production

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:27.600
privileges that a
workload can assume,

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:30.360
and ultimately the
production resources

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.300
that the workload can access.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:36.990
So the goal for
the deploy phase is

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:40.500
to ensure that only
trusted workload can assume

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.980
the right production identity.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.300
Then during the runtime
phase, making sure

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:50.820
that secure accesses are carried
out only by trusted production

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:52.730
identities.

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:56.040
If we take a closer look
at the deploy phase,

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:00.030
we realize that production
identities are typically

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:04.020
associated with a scope
to runtime environments,

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:07.230
such as the namespace
or a cluster.

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:09.630
Then the deploy
production security goal

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:15.150
can roughly be translated to
ensure only trusted workload

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:18.750
can be deployed
to the designated

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:21.670
trusted environments.

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:24.780
So what exactly is
a trusted workload?

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:31.040
So there are a common
set of risks associated

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:34.730
with deploying a workload.

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:40.220
The workload deployed may be
built from untrusted code,

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:44.630
in an untrusted environment, and
the typical mitigation for that

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:47.840
is to make sure it's
built centrally,

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:50.420
and also make sure
that is signed

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:52.760
by the centralized
process that views it,

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:57.000
so that it can be
verified later on.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.780
The workload may include
a vulnerable package.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:03.390
To help with that,
all images should

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.610
be scanned for no
vulnerabilities

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:07.470
before they are deployed.

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:09.750
But again, that
is not sufficient

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:13.260
because you have to act on
the vulnerability findings

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:16.020
by, again, signing
it, that verifies

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:21.210
that the vulnerability
scan bar has been reached

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:26.060
before a workload is deployed.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:29.520
And then what about workloads
that are not sufficiently

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:31.830
tested or reviewed?

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:36.120
Again, it is not sufficient
that the necessary tests

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:37.260
are performed.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:40.980
There should also be
evidences, such a signature

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:44.660
that a workload passes
all necessary tests.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:49.050
Once a workload is
approved for deployment,

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:53.230
it may still be deployed
with a wrong configuration,

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.470
such as wrong as root.

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:59.070
That is why it's important to
have configuration guardrails

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.620
to catch those mistakes.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:04.050
Last but not least,
the workload that

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:07.290
is approved for one
environment may not

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:10.410
be suitable for
the other, so it's

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:12.510
important to have
deploy policies that

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:16.050
are environmentally
appropriate, to only admit

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:20.320
workloads that are approved
for the target environment.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:24.700
In summary, if we put in
place all of these controls,

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:27.300
we should have
trusted workload that

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:32.670
are viewed from the right code,
through the right process,

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:35.310
and deployed with the
right configuration

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:36.770
to the right environment.

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:43.520
Anthos offers tools and
products to implement zero trust

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:45.800
deployments and
all of the controls

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:48.770
that we described in
the previous slide.

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:52.520
Customer can choose to use
Cloud Build, or other CI

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:57.620
tools to implement a centralized
lock down CSV process.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:00.290
And they can build
workload using

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:04.820
secure managed-base images
that are continuously

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:08.720
scanned and patched
for vulnerabilities.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:13.780
Once the image is viewed, and
it gets checked into the Google

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:16.460
Container Registry,
it gets picked up

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:19.790
by the vulnerability scanner.

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:23.540
Google also hosts a
contender analysis API

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:29.560
that serves as a
container knowledge base,

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:36.020
that stores metadatas, findings,
build records, test results,

00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:38.990
so that all the information
associated with a container

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:42.790
can be found in one place
for verification later on.

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:45.910
Once the image is
ready to be deployed,

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:50.950
a key part of the security
control is deploy policy.

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:54.640
Binary authorization
ensures that only explicitly

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:57.850
authorized image can be
deployed to the target runtime

00:11:57.850 --> 00:11:59.080
environment.

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:05.170
For example, customer can set
a policy for their PCI clusters

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:09.580
to only allow images
from a centralized

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:14.380
build pipeline that also meets
their vulnerability scanning

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:17.070
bars.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:20.030
Let's take a closer look
at binary authorization.

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:24.570
Binary authorization defines
an attestation framework

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:28.540
using signature verification.

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:32.310
So CSV components
can sign or attest

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:36.600
an image, as it passes
through each of the required

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:40.050
stages in the release process.

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:43.140
The user can then require
certain attestations

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.720
to be present at deploy
time, to make sure

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:48.900
that the required stages
in the release process

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:51.350
were actually carried out.

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:53.450
This mechanism
allows the customer

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:58.550
to control what can be deployed,
in addition to who can deploy.

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:02.120
One may have different policies
for each of the production

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:03.150
environments.

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:05.540
For example,
customers may require

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:08.120
images deployed to
the PCI environment

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:11.490
to be signed off by the
compliance stakeholders,

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:14.900
in addition to meeting the
regular test and quality

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:19.100
bars for regular
production environments.

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:21.420
In addition to
signature verification,

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:23.660
binary authorization
also supports

00:13:23.660 --> 00:13:28.930
an allow list for certain third
party images that are deployed

00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:31.420
in the user's environment.

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:35.270
The security team, for
example, can make it explicit,

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:38.020
which third party
images and versions

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:40.300
is allowed across
their organization,

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:45.980
so it makes it very easy
to manage and operate.

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:49.350
And then there are also
break glass processes,

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:52.800
in case there is a
production emergency

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:56.670
and the workload needs to
be pushed without satisfying

00:13:56.670 --> 00:14:00.660
the necessary release
requirement, in which case

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:02.920
the deployment will
be logged in autolog

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:05.360
and can be reviewed later on.

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:08.120
In the end, with the
binary authorization,

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:11.030
every workload that gets
released into the production

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:13.940
environment can
be accounted for.

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:17.270
It should be either
built by the customer

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:21.080
and signed by their centralized
builder and processors,

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:26.450
or is explicitly whitelisted
from vendor images,

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:28.790
or if is a Google
provided system

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:35.320
image, that is whitelisted by
default using a system policy.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:37.330
In addition to
binary authorization,

00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:40.750
here are additional
product features

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:43.890
of Anthos for secure deployment.

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:46.720
The Google Container Registry
offers a vulnerability

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:50.140
scanning feature that
provides detailed insights

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:55.180
for known vulnerabilities
for checking images.

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:57.760
Once the initial scan's
done, it continues

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:00.400
to perform incremental
scannings every time

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:02.530
the vulnerability is updated.

00:15:02.530 --> 00:15:04.630
User gets vulnerability
updates, even

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:08.150
for images that are
already running.

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:09.840
And those config
management makes

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:12.580
it easy to set up
configuration guardrails.

00:15:12.580 --> 00:15:16.410
So it offers both customizable
and building policies,

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:20.280
such as requiring strict
mTLS for all services

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:23.040
in a particular namespace.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:26.780
And then managed base images are
a set of container-based images

00:15:26.780 --> 00:15:29.580
host, I'll call it
marketplace, that

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:32.980
are maintained and
regularly patched by Google.

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:35.520
They're used for
Google's own products

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:38.400
and are a good place to start
when building application

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:40.810
containers.

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:44.770
Now that we have looked
over the product features,

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:47.630
let's do a quick recap.

00:15:47.630 --> 00:15:51.930
So we take a look at the
workload life cycle again.

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:56.140
Code from approved sources
are used in a locked down,

00:15:56.140 --> 00:16:00.790
centralized build process to
produce a workload container.

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:04.210
And the workload container
then get tested and scanned

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:06.760
for known vulnerabilities.

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:09.900
Then as part of
the release process

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:14.340
for that workload during
the deployment stage,

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:16.860
it gets verified by
final authorization

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:19.620
against deploy
policies, to ensure

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:22.020
that it meets the
customer requirements

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:25.560
and has come from
a trusted process.

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:27.750
At this point,
trust your workload

00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:31.110
has assumed the right
production identity

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:35.350
by running on the designated
production environment.

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:39.870
I'm now going to
hand over to Samrat

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:44.690
to describe how to secure this
workload while it's running.

00:16:44.690 --> 00:16:46.660
SAMRAT RAY: Thank you, Sandra.

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:52.950
We now have a trusted workload
that we can trust, in addition

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:54.560
to just trusting the network.

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:58.380
All right, this allows us
now to have a different basis

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:03.210
of trust, which is rooted in
the workload and its identity,

00:17:03.210 --> 00:17:06.869
rather than on just the network.

00:17:06.869 --> 00:17:10.710
We will now talk about how
we can leverage this workload

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:15.530
identity to securely grant
access to sensitive data

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:20.440
and to sensitive infrastructure
in our production environment.

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:25.380
So how do we trust
a production client?

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:27.869
The way to trust a
production client

00:17:27.869 --> 00:17:31.170
is to mitigate a number
of risks associated

00:17:31.170 --> 00:17:33.900
with access within a
production environment.

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:38.040
These risks include access to
payload by entities listening

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:40.410
on the production network.

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:44.220
It includes things like
untrusted workload,

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:47.850
replaying application
credentials

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:50.680
to gain access to data.

00:17:50.680 --> 00:17:52.680
All right, so some stolen
navigation credentials

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:54.438
can be used to then
gain access to data,

00:17:54.438 --> 00:17:56.230
which the workload is
not supposed to gain,

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:58.960
have access to.

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:02.340
And so another
risk that is there

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:06.570
is a client impersonating
a more privileged workload.

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:10.800
And similarly, a
compromised client

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:13.380
can not only impersonate
a privileged workload,

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:15.670
but a compromised
client can also

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:19.260
exfiltrate all user
data that it has access

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:22.440
to on its own privilege.

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:26.370
Lastly, an insider
can also gain access

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:30.090
to sensitive data and
sensitive backends

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:32.430
without going through
appropriate checks

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:34.920
by bypassing certain frontends.

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:38.160
So all of these risks
exist in terms of access

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:40.350
within a production network.

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:42.060
So the corresponding
set of controls

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:44.330
that we can use to
mitigate these risks

00:18:44.330 --> 00:18:47.850
are, we can ensure that
all workload to workload

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:51.265
communication is encrypted in
transit to prevent entities

00:18:51.265 --> 00:18:52.640
that are listening
on the network

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:54.420
from gaining access
to sensitive data,

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:56.520
and credentials, et cetera.

00:18:56.520 --> 00:18:59.340
We can ensure that all
clients are explicitly

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:02.460
authorized, such that untrusted
clients don't gain access,

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:05.580
even if they have the right
application level credentials.

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:09.000
We want to ensure that
when we authorize a client,

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:12.450
we are authorizing it based
upon a non-replayable attribute

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:14.460
of the client,
versus identifying

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:16.380
the client through
something like a token

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:18.390
that itself can be replayed.

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:22.530
Thus we want to prevent
impersonations by using

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:28.350
something like an IP address or
a credential that is based upon

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:32.550
a cryptographically signed
certificate, for example,

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:34.750
that cannot be replayed.

00:19:34.750 --> 00:19:38.340
The next is we want to
ensure that in the case

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:43.440
there is a client that can
access PII on behalf of an end

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:48.570
user, it can only do so when it
is acting on behalf of that end

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:51.540
user, and not when
if it is compromised

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:54.600
that it can gain access
to PII without a valid end

00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:59.510
user that actually has
authority on that data.

00:19:59.510 --> 00:20:03.070
And lastly, we want to ensure
that all insider access are

00:20:03.070 --> 00:20:06.100
logged down to authorized
clients that can implement

00:20:06.100 --> 00:20:09.430
the right set of
checks and obfuscation,

00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:12.510
et cetera, before
granting access to data

00:20:12.510 --> 00:20:15.000
to a privileged insider.

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:17.970
Thus we will now
talk about how we

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:25.660
can implement these set of
control mechanisms with Anthos.

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:29.800
A key tool for implementing
these principles

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:33.070
that we just talked about
is Anthos Service Mesh.

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:36.310
We'll walk you through how
Anthos Service Mesh can

00:20:36.310 --> 00:20:41.020
help you secure workload access,
followed by access to data,

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:45.530
and to mitigate privileged
insider risks as well.

00:20:45.530 --> 00:20:48.310
So the first step is to ensure
that you can secure workload

00:20:48.310 --> 00:20:51.850
access without taking a
dependency on the security

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:53.440
of the underlying network.

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:55.810
This does not mean that
you are not securing

00:20:55.810 --> 00:20:57.630
the underlying network.

00:20:57.630 --> 00:21:00.910
We still recommend that you
have the right kind of parameter

00:21:00.910 --> 00:21:06.190
security, and have a
minimum set of segmentation

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:08.025
for [INAUDIBLE] out
access, ensuring

00:21:08.025 --> 00:21:10.150
that your backends are not
exposed to the internet,

00:21:10.150 --> 00:21:11.960
for example.

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:15.700
Having said that, once you
do that it, as we described

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:21.010
before, that is not sufficient
for modern production

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:22.100
environments.

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:24.880
So thus, we now will talk
about how you will actually

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:28.900
grant access within your
production network context.

00:21:28.900 --> 00:21:33.850
So the first step is
to ensure that we only

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:37.180
grant access to
authorized network peers,

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:38.640
to authorized clients.

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:41.740
And we recommend
doing that using

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:44.100
a certificate-based identity.

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:46.510
The advantage of a
certificate-based identity is

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:51.220
that it is not replayable,
because the receiver

00:21:51.220 --> 00:21:54.040
of a certificate cannot
replay it without also being

00:21:54.040 --> 00:21:55.930
in possession of
the private keys.

00:21:55.930 --> 00:21:59.650
Thus, it prevents
impersonation attacks.

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:02.770
And the use of mutual
TLS for authentication

00:22:02.770 --> 00:22:04.530
also implies that
you get encryption

00:22:04.530 --> 00:22:08.620
in transit, another key property
that we described earlier.

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:11.290
The value of Anthos Service
Mesh in this context

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:15.400
is to automatically manage
your certificates and keys

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:20.550
for tens of thousands of
workloads in your production.

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:26.480
The next step is to ensure
that you use an authorization

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:29.930
policy that can reason
about these identities,

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:32.420
and in addition
to that, can also

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:34.430
reason about
additional attributes,

00:22:34.430 --> 00:22:38.450
like namespace, and IPs, as
needed in order to grant access

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:39.520
to a network peer.

00:22:39.520 --> 00:22:42.380
Anthos Service Mesh
provide such a policy that

00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:44.730
is integrated into the Mesh.

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:49.660
All of this implies that your
security can be policy-driven

00:22:49.660 --> 00:22:51.750
and is not baked
into the code, thus

00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:54.810
your application developers
can focus on business logic

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:57.060
rather than on security.

00:22:57.060 --> 00:23:00.490
The next step, after
having secured workload

00:23:00.490 --> 00:23:03.780
to workload access, is to
secure access to user data

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:05.580
by a workload.

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:08.820
So in addition to ensuring
that you have the right client,

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:11.550
in order to gain
access to user data,

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:15.270
we want to ensure that the
client is accessing on behalf

00:23:15.270 --> 00:23:17.670
of the right end user.

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:22.500
And we can do this by ensuring
that the client presents

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:26.910
a valid end user
credential that has

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:32.080
the right kind of claims to
access the data on the backend.

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:34.170
Again, Anthos Service
Mesh provides you

00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:37.330
with a policy engine
that can A, validate

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:39.790
that that is a valid
end user credential that

00:23:39.790 --> 00:23:42.340
is being presented,
with the right signer,

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:46.300
and that actually, has
not expired, for example.

00:23:46.300 --> 00:23:53.530
And it can also reason about the
claims in that JWT credential,

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:57.320
to ensure that the credential
has the right kind of claim

00:23:57.320 --> 00:24:02.200
so that the data can be
accessed, based upon what

00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:05.590
the end user has
access to, and not

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:08.670
on the privilege of the
credit card front end itself.

00:24:11.330 --> 00:24:14.960
The last piece that Anthos
Service Mesh helps you secure

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:17.030
is insider access.

00:24:17.030 --> 00:24:20.390
With respect to insider
access, Anthos Service Mesh

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:24.170
ensures that your insider
can only gain access

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:28.970
to the right frontend, because
Anthos Service Mesh will grant

00:24:28.970 --> 00:24:33.860
access to data, let's say,
based upon a valid end user

00:24:33.860 --> 00:24:36.650
credential, and your
credit card backend

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:38.510
will grant access
to data if it's

00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:40.380
a valid privileged insider.

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:44.390
But at the Mesh layer, you can
ensure that this valid insider

00:24:44.390 --> 00:24:48.920
is actually accessing the
data via the right frontend,

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:52.250
such as the case tool over
here, which could implement

00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:56.760
obfuscation of PII as needed.

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:59.100
It will deny access of the
same privileged insider

00:24:59.100 --> 00:25:01.640
where to deploy their
own personal script,

00:25:01.640 --> 00:25:04.250
and then tried to gain
access to data without going

00:25:04.250 --> 00:25:05.660
through the keys too.

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:08.930
A key principle
for granting access

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:12.590
to sensitive information on
infrastructure to an insider

00:25:12.590 --> 00:25:15.770
is to ensure that we strongly
authenticate the insider.

00:25:15.770 --> 00:25:18.620
And this is done in
Google Cloud Platform

00:25:18.620 --> 00:25:21.230
through a product called
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:25:21.230 --> 00:25:25.280
Identity-Aware Proxy users
BeyondCorp principles to ensure

00:25:25.280 --> 00:25:30.050
that the user identity
is authenticated based

00:25:30.050 --> 00:25:33.470
on multiple factors, such
as the client device,

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:35.490
attributes, and IP address.

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:39.650
For example, you can ensure
that you only grant access

00:25:39.650 --> 00:25:44.810
to sensitive data when the
insider accesses the case

00:25:44.810 --> 00:25:48.410
tool from a well-managed
corporate device that

00:25:48.410 --> 00:25:50.380
is secure.

00:25:50.380 --> 00:25:55.900
This ensures that you're
only granting access

00:25:55.900 --> 00:25:58.690
to the right person,
via the right frontend,

00:25:58.690 --> 00:26:03.230
coming from the right
endpoint device, for example.

00:26:03.230 --> 00:26:07.660
So to summarize, Anthos
provides features

00:26:07.660 --> 00:26:10.360
for securing access
in productions based

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:12.320
upon zero trust principles.

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:13.880
The first being
Anthos Service Mesh,

00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:16.720
which provides mutual TLS
for encryption in transit

00:26:16.720 --> 00:26:18.610
and for a stronger
authentication

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:20.200
and authorization.

00:26:20.200 --> 00:26:24.040
Anthos Service Mesh allows
you to secure access

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:27.310
across clusters and
across VPC networks.

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:30.310
It also allows you
to secure access

00:26:30.310 --> 00:26:36.320
based upon end user context, and
not just on the peer context.

00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:40.010
Identity-Aware Proxy
enables you to grant

00:26:40.010 --> 00:26:45.080
access to your insiders based
upon strong authentication

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:47.690
of the end user
itself, and based

00:26:47.690 --> 00:26:53.310
upon things like the device
context and IP address,

00:26:53.310 --> 00:26:54.230
for example.

00:26:54.230 --> 00:26:56.300
It's integrated with
Anthos Service Mesh,

00:26:56.300 --> 00:26:58.750
and it can also support
third party identities,

00:26:58.750 --> 00:27:01.130
so you can bring your
own identity provider

00:27:01.130 --> 00:27:03.380
and use that with
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:27:03.380 --> 00:27:05.930
Lastly, one of the things that
we did not talk about before

00:27:05.930 --> 00:27:07.910
is Kubernetes network policy.

00:27:07.910 --> 00:27:11.990
It is still important in this
context of zero trust access

00:27:11.990 --> 00:27:15.350
because it provides you with
the segmentation technology that

00:27:15.350 --> 00:27:17.530
works within a single cluster.

00:27:17.530 --> 00:27:20.480
So thus, you can use something
like Kubernetes network policy

00:27:20.480 --> 00:27:25.320
to implement a policy
that says the frontend,

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:28.550
which is internet-facing, cannot
directly access the backend

00:27:28.550 --> 00:27:30.940
by bypassing the middle tier.

00:27:30.940 --> 00:27:33.560
Right, so this kind of a
[INAUDIBLE] segmentation

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:36.980
can be implemented with the
Kubernetes network policy,

00:27:36.980 --> 00:27:38.820
and it adds defense
in depth on top

00:27:38.820 --> 00:27:43.310
of what you have at the base
infrastructure security level,

00:27:43.310 --> 00:27:45.840
and the security that you
implement using Anthos Service

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:48.310
Mesh.

00:27:48.310 --> 00:27:54.080
So, to summarize zero
trust production security

00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:57.950
can be achieved with
three principles, which

00:27:57.950 --> 00:28:00.890
is only trusted code and
configuration can be deployed

00:28:00.890 --> 00:28:03.080
to production,
trust is associated

00:28:03.080 --> 00:28:07.130
with workload and user, and not
just with the network location,

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:10.760
and all access to sensitive
data and infrastructure

00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:13.490
is granted to the
right network peers,

00:28:13.490 --> 00:28:17.160
and under the
right user context.

00:28:17.160 --> 00:28:18.820
Thank you.

